<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raghavanka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raghavanka was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> period, a nephew and pupil of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara">Harihara</a>. His Harishchandra Kavya, retelling the story of the truthful king Harishchandra, was the first major work in the shatpadi, six-line, metre, which became a standard form of Kannada poetry.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raghavanka was a writer and poet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> court in the 13th century. He was the son of Rudrani, the sister of the Shaiva poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara">Harihara</a>, and grew up as his uncle's protégé and pupil. Like Harihara he was a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and most of his writing celebrates Shaiva saints.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His major work, the Harishchandra Kavya, dated to about 1200 or 1225, tells the life of King Harishchandra in the shatpadi metre of six-line verses. It brings out the clash of personalities in lively dialogue, between the sages Vishvamitra and Vashishta, between Harishchandra and Vishvamitra, and between the king and the dancing girls, and narrates Harishchandra's fidelity to truth and his redemption after he is rescued by an untouchable he had once rejected. One elegiac passage, in the mandanila ragele metre of rhymed couplets, is the lament of Chandramati over her young son Lohitashva, killed by a snake while gathering firewood for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> master. L. S. Sheshagiri Rao of the Sahitya Akademi held that no other language treats the story in this way.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harihara">Harihara</a> rejected the work. According to legend he was appalled that a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> should praise an ordinary mortal, and five of Raghavanka's teeth fell out; to atone, Raghavanka wrote five works praising saints, and the teeth returned one by one. These are the Siddharama charitra, on the 12th-century saint Siddharama of Sonnalige, the Somanatha charitra, on Somayya of Puligere and his conversion of a Jain temple into a Shiva temple, the Viresvara charita, on the warrior Virabhadra, and the Hariharamahatva and Sarabha charitra, both now considered lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The shatpadi metre had been used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> before him, but Raghavanka made it popular, and its flexibility was taken up by generations of later poets, Shaiva and Vaishnava alike. The Harishchandra Kavya is counted among the classics of Kannada literature, and Chandramati's lament has remained popular for centuries, recited by Gamakis, who narrate stories to music.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raghavanka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period was also rich in Kannada literature: Harihara and <b>Raghavanka</b> pioneered new metres and themes, Janna and Rudrabhatta wrote major works on Jain and Vaishnava subjects, and Kesiraja’s Shabdamanidarpana of 1260 became the classic grammar of Old Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hampi, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.34,76.46&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Hampi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6638 1593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raghavanka"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7088 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7088 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.34,76.46&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
